## Write custom plot function 

-  The plot should create a boxplot of the numeric variable at the levels of the categorical variable
-  It should take one numerical and one caterorical variable as inputs 


Before that let's construct a label query so that I can display it on the boxplot 

```{r}


get_label_for_var <- function(var) {
  
  variables_metadata <- load_variables_rds_by_year("2024") #let's use default 2024
  #I also checked ST and STATE variables between different years the labels are same 
  var_label <- variables_metadata[[var]]$label
  if (is.null(var_label)) {
    var_label <- var   # "year" has no metadata entry -- fall back to the raw name
  }

  var_label
}

```

Implementing the actual `plot` function 

```{r}
plot.census <- function(x, numeric_var, categorical_var) {
  if(missing(numeric_var) || missing(categorical_var)) {
    stop("both numeric_var and categorical_var are required")
  }
  
  allowed_categorical_vars <- c("FER", "HHL", "SCH", "SCHL", "SEX","year")
  allowed_numeric_vars <- c("AGEP", "GASP", "GRPIP",  "JWMNP")
  
  numeric_var <- match_allowed_value(numeric_var, allowed_numeric_vars)
  if(is.na(numeric_var)) {
    stop("numeric_var must be one of: ", paste(allowed_numeric_vars, collapse = ","))
  }
  col_names_tibble <- colnames(x)
  
  if(!(numeric_var %in% col_names_tibble)) {
    stop("numeric_var is not part of available tibble columns: ", paste(col_names_tibble, collapse = ","))
  }
  
  categorical_var <- match_allowed_value(categorical_var, allowed_categorical_vars)
  if(is.na(categorical_var)) {
    stop("categorical_var must be one of: ", paste(allowed_categorical_vars, collapse = ","))
  }
  
   if(!(categorical_var %in% col_names_tibble)) {
    stop("categorical_var is not part of available tibble columns: ", paste(col_names_tibble, collapse = ","))
   }
  
  ggplot(x, aes(x = get(categorical_var), y = get(numeric_var), weight = PWGTP)) + 
    geom_boxplot() + 
    labs(x = paste0(categorical_var, "(", get_label_for_var(categorical_var), ")"),
         y = paste0(numeric_var, "(", get_label_for_var(numeric_var), ")"),
         title = paste(numeric_var, " by ", categorical_var),
         subtitle = "Weighted by PWGTP (survey weight)",
         caption = "Source: Census PUMS data")
  
}

```

Now let's test the `plot` function

First lets plot AGEP boxplots by SEX

```{r}
plot(pums_combined_tibble,"AGEP","SEX")
```

First lets plot GRPIP boxplots by SEX

```{r}
plot(pums_combined_tibble,"GRPIP","SEX")
```

# Investigation
I am going to use both the above boxplots as means of investigations and to draw some conclusions

## Investigation-1 AGEP vs SEX
But first let's use AGEP vs SEX
In the AGEP VS SEX boxplot, it shows that population age is similar between both sexes, although the mean age is slightly different between 2 sexes. Also there are no outliers the distribution itself is from 0-90+ years. I expected to see outliers in this plot given we know that there are super-agers. 

Let's summarize AGEP numerical variable and see if it provides additional information on AGEP value distribution.

```{r}
summary(pums_combined_tibble,numeric_vars = "AGEP", categorical_vars = "SEX")

```

Summary doesn't provide much insight into outliers. The concept that I am trying to check it whether there are any values for AGEP variable for either sexes that `ourside 1.5 * IQR from the edges of IQR`

```{r}

pums_combined_tibble |>
  group_by(SEX) |>
  summarize(
    q1 = quantile(AGEP,0.25, na.rm = TRUE),
    q3 = quantile(AGEP, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE),
    iqr = q3-q1,
    upper_fence = q3 + 1.5 * iqr,
    lower_fence = q1 - 1.5 * iqr,
    actual_max = max(AGEP, na.rm = TRUE),
    actual_min = min(AGEP, na.rm = TRUE),
    has_high_outliers = actual_max > upper_fence,
    has_low_outliers = actual_min < lower_fence 
  )
```

Now this is perfect, we mathematically confirmed that there are no outliers in the age distributions of the population (for state = 37)

Another thing `summary` function provided is male to female ratio for the data we are reviewing (years = 2021-2024 and for state = 37)

```{r}
213464/226293 # 0.943308
```

This observation matches with what I found on internet [Population Pyramids -North Carolina](https://www.populationpyramids.org/states/north-carolina)

## Investigation-2 GRPIP vs SEX 
One thing that stands out immediately in GRPIP vs SEX plot is the lower fence of IQR actually starts at `0`

This means there are people who do not pay rent at all and their share of NC population is more than 25 %. Per boxplot definition since at least 25% of the population pays no rent at all, the 25th percentile (Q1) of GRPIP is exactly 0. Because GRPIP can't go below 0, there is no room for a lower whisker to extend. It collapses to the same point as the box edge.

Now let's run the similar checks as AGEP vs SEX to confirm this findings. While at it, we can also observe high outliers 

```{r}

pums_combined_tibble |>
  group_by(SEX) |>
  summarize(
    q1 = quantile(GRPIP,0.25, na.rm = TRUE),
    q3 = quantile(GRPIP, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE),
    iqr = q3-q1,
    upper_fence = q3 + 1.5 * iqr,
    lower_fence = q1 - 1.5 * iqr,
    actual_max = max(GRPIP, na.rm = TRUE),
    actual_min = min(GRPIP, na.rm = TRUE),
    has_high_outliers = actual_max > upper_fence,
    has_low_outliers = actual_min < lower_fence 
  )
```

Now this confirms the presense of high outliers where the some population of NC is paying more rent than the income they receive. A generally recommended rule is rental expense cannot be more than 30-40% of income. Here clearly there is a segment where this is not holding true. Let's check summary to see if it offers any additional perspective.


```{r}
summary(pums_combined_tibble,numeric_vars = "GRPIP", categorical_vars = "SEX")

```

let's see if the GRPIP changes by Year

```{r}
summary(pums_combined_tibble,numeric_vars = "GRPIP", categorical_vars = "Year")
plot(pums_combined_tibble,numeric_var = "GRPIP", categorical_var = "Year")


```

The rent situation hasn't improved much over the years per this plot


## Investigation-3 JWMNP vs year

```{r}
summary(pums_combined_tibble,numeric_vars = "JWMNP", categorical_vars = "year")
plot(pums_combined_tibble,numeric_var = "JWMNP", categorical_var = "year")

```

```{r}

pums_combined_tibble |>
  group_by(year) |>
  summarize(
    median_jwmnp = median(JWMNP, na.rm = TRUE),
    pct_zero = mean(JWMNP == 0, na.rm = TRUE),
    q1 = quantile(JWMNP,0.25, na.rm = TRUE),
    q3 = quantile(JWMNP, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE),
    iqr = q3-q1,
    upper_fence = q3 + 1.5 * iqr,
    lower_fence = q1 - 1.5 * iqr,
    actual_max = max(JWMNP, na.rm = TRUE),
    actual_min = min(JWMNP, na.rm = TRUE),
    has_high_outliers = actual_max > upper_fence,
    has_low_outliers = actual_min < lower_fence 
  )
```
The Q1 for all the years is actually '0' indicating these are either remote workers, workers staying next door to work, and people without jobs. 
While mean stayed around 10 minutes, the max value of outliers has increased gradually from 2021 to 2024. This suggests more workers moving to places that are far from work locations during the pandemic-remote-was-the-norm time. However they were called to work (RTO) which resulted in increased travel time. In the investigation pct_zero column clearly shows gradual decrease from 64.5% to 61.5%

